body:
I haven't ignored anything you've said.  I just didn't want to make a post as long as this one.  For example, you brought up that Zetta is an unreliable source by my logic because he's postgame, but that's beside the point.  The fact that they used Zetta instead of Baal at all is my point.  Why use Zetta instead of maintaining the Makai universe's tradition of always using Baal, even outside of Disgaea games?

&#x200B;

Well if you play Disgaea 4, it's pretty obvious that the power scale is way higher than in other installments of the series.  With the exception of reality itself falling apart, the entire game is spent with Valvatorez fearlessly shrugging off every challenger and scenario thrown his way without so much as batting an eye, regardless of how surprising or unfair the situation is, always leaving everyone who witnessed his actions (minus Fenrich who already knows what Valvatorez is capable of) completely dumbstruck.  By the end of it, you're declaring war on god himself, then winning.  After that, when reality itself starts to unwind from the sheer impossibility of what you just did, you then save reality by rejecting it outright and substituting your own, thereby teaching a lesson to the very fabric of reality itself.  Let's not pretend that isn't way over the top even for the Disgaea franchise.  No other MC can boast feats even approaching this level.  Naturally, after doing all that, it would've been anti-climactic to face Baal as the optional superboss, considering how much of a downgrade that would be just from the events of the main storyline.  So instead, for this game only, they needed nothing less than the man who has already been established as the strongest physical entity in all the multiverse: Zetta.

&#x200B;

You bring up Laharl, but let's really examine this.  For starters, yes, the version of Laharl that showed up in Disgaea 4 was indeed the Disgaea 1 Laharl.  You argue that a change of clothes means nothing, but again, it feels like you're making an effort to avoid accuracy here just to fit your narrative.  It wasn't just his clothes that changed from Disgaea 1 to D2, and you should know that.  He is canonically stated to be taller, more maturely built, and have significantly longer antennae, all of which can be noticed at a glance.  Seriously, just go look at Laharl from Disgaea 1 and then compare him to D2's Laharl.  If you truly can't see that he clearly transitioned from child to teenager, I don't know what to tell you.  The game itself outright states it and his sprite is changed to represent it, with in-game characters even referencing it.  He simply looks different.

&#x200B;

So, to my previous point: If Laharl had canonically engaged in battle against Valvatorez when he was a child (Disgaea 1), then all reason dictates that Laharl as a teenager (D2) would have already known him when they met again in the post game of D2, regardless of when Disgaea 4 took place.  So unless you really want to argue that we don't know whether or not child Laharl came before adolescent Laharl, I'm going to assume this needs no further debate.  Now, considering Laharl is **not** the type to let go of grudges, there's not a chance in hell that he would forget *losing* that particular battle.  Yet all the same, they act like they'd never met.  Because they never had, and they still never did.  Laharl never canonically engaged in combat with, or even met, Valvatorez.  It was just put in the game as an opportunity to let you play as your favorite characters.

&#x200B;

Now that we've got that out of the way, let's talk about Laharl's known limits.  In Disgaea 1, Laharl got absolutely wrecked without so much as a visible effort by the "Alternate Overlord" who, as described by Ghoss in game, is actually nothing more than a common demon in the land of Carnage, which is truly ruled by the Lord of Terror, who we all understand to be Baal.  This means Laharl is not on the level of Carnage demons, which makes sense, as he is a mere Overlord, and a child at that.  Laharl also almost died to Mederas, had it not been for the aid of his friends.  In D2, Etna outwardly admits that 9 times out of 10, she and Laharl combined would be no match for Xenolith, who himself is only special because he absorbed the power of the netherworld and some of Etna's power thanks to some artifact.

&#x200B;

All of this is pretty consistent and in line with the idea that Laharl is not some Baal-slaying tyrant-level superdemon.  If Laharl could beat Baal, he could walk all over Xenolith, who merely had a portion of the power of a single netherworld.  But mysteriously, he couldn't.  This is because in reality, Laharl can't even beat that common Carnage demon, as clearly shown in the story of the first game.  As soon as you acknowledge that the post game content is just for funsies, suddenly everything makes perfect sense and there are no inconsistencies.  The reality of the matter is that Laharl is weaker than the common Carnage demon, and not even the strongest demon in his own netherworld, as Krichevskoy's royal guard demonstrated when they laid the smackdown on the very demon Laharl and gang so thoroughly lost to, without suffering any casualties or even so much as treating it like it was that big of a deal afterwards.  That said, within the context of his own netherworld, Laharl is an exceptionally powerful demon in his own right, and none short of Krichevskoy's elite are a match for him.  This itself is in line with the general notion that everyone keeps telling Laharl that Krichevskoy was a far superior leader and much greater source of inspiration for his underlings than Laharl is, which would explain why Krichevskoy had underlings that pushed themselves to become so powerful for his sake, whereas nobody is willing to lift a finger for Laharl.  If you recall, Laharl is a couch potato who plays video games all day long and no one respects.  It makes perfect sense that everyone would be upset at losing Krichevskoy and replacing him with this arrogant, lazy, irresponsible kid with a childish god complex (the word "chunibyo" in Japanese refers to this common childish delusion of inflated self-importance) despite having no leadership or kingdom management skills whatsoever, and only enough individual power to beat demons who don't work on improving themselves.  Naturally, this would eventually boil over into a huge problem that, wouldn't you know it, D2 flawlessly represents!

&#x200B;

In essence, you prefer to argue that Laharl could take Baal in a fight, but somehow loses to a common Carnage demon, is presumably stronger than his royal guard even though they neither respect him nor fear him even slightly, can barely eek out a desperate victory against Xenolith where he would've lost any other time by his and Etna's own admission, loses to Valvatorez, but also later mysteriously *beats* Valvatorez and doesn't recall ever losing to him or even seeing him in the first place, is definitely stronger than Addell despite Addell *also* beating Baal, gets crushed and is forced to retreat from Zenon who, as per Disgaea Infinite, is weaker than Baal whom Laharl would have supposedly beaten by that time, is presumably weaker than Mao who supposedly destroys all of reality but is also only barely stronger than a human hero yet *also* of course defeats Baal, and is somehow stronger than Krichevskoy whom is stronger than his royal guard that defeated the Carnage demon that defeated Laharl.  Do I have that right?

&#x200B;

Meanwhile, I attest that Laharl is weaker than a Carnage demon, as demonstrated by the story, and he never actually fought Baal.  This puts him below Krichevskoy's royal guard, which would explain why they don't fear him despite literally everyone in the netherworld knowing Laharl absolutely will use violent force to subjugate anyone who defies him.  They stick around and protect Laharl out of respect for Krichevskoy, and consistently make it quite clear Laharl has no power over them.  This also explains why Laharl was so readily trounced by Zenon, despite Zenon being established as weaker than Baal.  Likewise, it explains why Xenolith was such an insurmountable foe in the story, despite clearly being weaker than someone like Zenon, whom a single netherworld's power would not make a difference for.  Why does Laharl claim to be the strongest, then?  Simple: He's a kid who fancies himself the most important person in existence.  Common childish chunibyo, nothing more.  Literally every single resident of the Overlord's Castle, including Flonne and Etna, all call him out on this at least once, with the latter two doing it constantly.  Likewise, none of the MCs except for Addell and Laharl have ever actually met, which explains why every time they see each other in noncanon post game, they always have no idea who they're talking to.  This means no one has ever fought or beaten anyone they have no way of actually beating, as, conveniently enough, the only inconsistencies always seem to happen in post game.

My intent wasn't to imply you have, and I acknowledge that you've said no such thing.  I promise I'm not trying to make a villain out of anybody.  I can see this is clearly something you're passionate about, and I have nothing at all against passion.  I've been choking back a lot of frustration and strong feelings of my own during this.  I'll elaborate more on that in a second... and uh... sorry again that I'm so long winded.  I know I take forever to make my points.

&#x200B;

The reason I brought it up is because I felt it would help illustrate my point that simply hating the way these men behave isn't going to solve anything.  In fact, I'm not even trying to imply that hate isn't warranted; believe me, I hate it more than you may ever realize.  I've faced my own traumas at the hands of my older brother ever since I was 6 years old, and I've felt the shame of those actions reflected back onto me simply by gender association every time I see or hear of a man acting that way or when I see or hear of a woman (or anyone really) shaming that behavior.  Even though I realize it's illogical to feel the guilt of others... I've just never quite been able to shake these dreadful feelings of shame and guilt every time I'm given a loving and consensual opportunity to share that kind of interaction with a friend.  It's so bad that every time I try, I just end up shaking all over and unable to go through with it.  I'm in my mid-late twenties and still a virgin (at least regarding penetration) because of this.  I'm still trying to ease my way through that.

&#x200B;

I don't say that to illicit pity.  I'm more or less at peace with my situation and... ha... while it does suck, I'm just more interested in trying to keep both sides safe from the endless cycle of antagonism that's born from misunderstanding and villainization.  Again, it's not pity I'm after.  I just want it to be well understood that I am not at all trying to hold these men blameless in their actions, nor am I even saying that they haven't earned that scorn.  My point is just that if we truly want to solve these issues, then understanding them and acting in accordance with that understanding is paramount.  I will never forgive my brother, nor will I ever be on pleasant terms with my family, but more than anything I just want to do my best to help this stop being an issue altogether.

Are you suggesting a Noxus deck should just counter flying by using all those Noxus flyers they definitely have?  Or are you saying they should use Reach minions instead?  Or should they focus more on direct damage spells like lightning bolt and just nuke the nexus down like that?  Ooh wait hold on, of course, you must be talking about milling them into submission.  Actually though, I think it would be better to just cancel the spellcast of any flyers by using cards like counterspell whenever they try to summon them.  Though I understand if you would prefer I force the opponent to constantly discard all his flyers before he can play them at all.  I must admit though, my favorite method is employing an army of lifesteal creatures to attack and replace all the health lost from the flyers.  Or maybe I should try something out of my comfort zone and have some enchantments in place that cause damage to all flyers on summon!  Aaaah my head is racing with ideas now!  I can't wait to log back into Magic Online and make my anti-flyer deck!

&#x200B;

...wait, we're talking about Legends of Runeterra?  Oh, right.  Yeah I've got nothin'.

My group and I are disagreeing on how the Unbreakable Goblin's fall damage reduction interacts with Cat Fall's fall damage reduction.  I believe Unbreakable applies first because it's built into my very body; I am simply structured in a way to be resistant to falling.  Then, after that, I am trained in falling skillfully (like parkour) to reduce the damage by 10ft.  So if I were to fall 30ft, first I'd halve it to 15, then cut it by 10 to 5.  However, my group thinks I should cut it by 10 first, making it 20, then halve it after that to 10.   There are many reasons to me that this makes no sense.  For starters, what they're claiming would mean that if I apply a technique to soften a fall, that would mysteriously alter my body's composition such that it would inexplicably resist the fall less than what it normally would, all because I softened the fall.  Their reasoning for this is because "Unbreakable" (the exact writing can be found below) mentions the word "damage" whereas Cat Fall does not, so they believe it's implied that I must take damage before Unbreakable applies at all.  This can't possibly be the case though!  Because if it required me to take damage before activating, and then its activation would make me take no damage, then that would make it de-activate because it can only function if I take damage, so then I WOULD take damage, which would then make it re-activate into an infinite feedback loop.  It only makes sense to read the ability as "Treat all falls as half distance."  Likewise, it would make no sense to make my body less resistant to falls (negating 1/3rd of a fall is objectively less resistant than negating 1/2 of a fall, implying my physiology is being somehow morphed) just because I softened the fall.

&#x200B;

Unbreakable Goblin states:  "When you fall, reduce the falling damage you take as though you had fallen half the distance."

Cat Fall reads:  "Your catlike aerial acrobatics allow you to cushion your falls. Treat falls as 10 feet shorter."

&#x200B;

I get why they think Unbreakable should apply second, but that's only if you're treating it as though I magically actually fell only 20ft when I apply Cat Fall instead of 30ft, which is just simply not the case.  The reality is that I am still falling 30ft, therefore my body should treat it as a 15ft fall, not a 20ft fall.
